    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499188/clinical-educator-dentistry-oral-health-casual-opportunity": {
      "title": "Clinical Educator  (Dentistry / Oral Health) - Casual Opportunity",
      "description": "Clinical Educator (Dentistry / Oral Health) - Casual Opportunity Reference no: 499188 Work type: Casual Location: Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Wagga Wagga Categories: Dentistry Flexible casual hours to fit your schedule Opportunity to contribute to rural health education and make a difference in regional communities Work within a supportive team committed to excellence in teaching and clinical practice The Opportunity Casual / Sessional (on an as needs basis) Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange and Wagga Wagga Join Charles Sturt University as a Clinical Educator on a casual basis and play a pivotal role in shaping the future of dental and oral health professionals. Based in one of our regional Dental and Oral Health Clinics, you’ll provide hands-on clinical supervision and mentorship to students, bridging academic learning with real-world practice. This role supports the development of the dental workforce in rural and regional communities while offering opportunities for professional growth and academic engagement. About you Full registration as a dental practitioner with the Australian Health Practitioner Regulation Agency (Ahpra). Proven ability to empathise effectively and tailor teaching approaches to accommodate diverse learning styles while following the principles of good clinical supervision Willingness to adapt their teaching schedule as needed to facilitate collaboration with colleagues at varying times. A strong desire to contribute to advancing dental education, actively contributing to the institution’s educational goals, and deriving fulfilment from teaching and mentoring future dental professionals About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Faculty of Science and Health here . Learn more about the School of Dentistry and Medical Sciences here . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply To express your interest for this role, please submit a cover letter that tells us more about you and attach your CV via email to ClinicalOps-DOHC@csu.edu.au Please note, registering your interest does not guarantee that an offer of employment will be made. This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Further information Additional information is available in the position description or by contacting: Jessica Mullins| Acting Clinical Operations Coordinator| ClinicalOps-DOHC@csu.edu.au | Ph: (02) 6365 7070 Closing Date: 11pm, 30 November 2026 Note applications for this casual role is open until the closing date specified above, however applications will be reviewed on need basis before the closing date. Advertised: 12 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 30 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499178/chief-information-and-digital-officer": {
      "title": "Chief Information and Digital Officer",
      "description": "Chief Information and Digital Officer Reference no: 499178 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Executive, Information Technology Lead the digital future of Australia's leading rural and regional university Shape the technology, data and digital capabilities that will underpin our next decade Strengthen cyber security, resilience and trust in a rapidly changing digital environment Charles Sturt University is embarking on a decade of significant reform. We want excellence across everything we do, a new direction for our teaching, learning and research, and a clear ambition to strengthen our role as Australia's leading rural and regional university. Technology and digital capability will be central to achieving that ambition. We are seeking a visible, influential and collaborative Chief Information and Digital Officer to shape and deliver the University's digital strategy through to 2030 and beyond. This is an opportunity to reset our digital foundations while establishing the capabilities needed for the next generation of teaching, research and university services. The opportunity Reporting to the Chief Operating Officer, the Chief Information and Digital Officer will lead the Division of Information Technology, comprising approximately 150 people across a multi-campus university. You will work closely with the Provost, Deputy Vice-Chancellor Research and other senior leaders to translate University strategy into a clear, prioritised technology and digital roadmap. You will lead a significant, multi-year transformation program focused on: Modernising our core platforms across HR, finance, research and identity, creating a simpler, more connected digital environment for students and staff. Strengthening cyber security and digital resilience , ensuring the University can manage evolving threats while maintaining trusted and reliable services. Leading responsible adoption of AI and automation , improving productivity, decision-making and the experience of students and staff while managing privacy, security and emerging risks. Establishing strong enterprise architecture and delivery capability , enabling digital services to scale, integrate and evolve. Building strategic partnerships with industry and technology providers to accelerate capability, innovation and value for the University. You will have accountability for ICT strategy and performance, financial stewardship, governance, regulatory compliance and the delivery of secure, reliable and human-centred digital services. You will also provide strategic advice to the University Executive, Council and relevant committees, bringing a clear understanding of technology, risk and digital opportunity to institutional decision-making. As a senior leader, you'll build a high-performing team and a culture of collaboration, innovation and continuous improvement. That culture will reflect our commitment to high trust and high accountability. About you You are an experienced technology and digital executive who can move confidently between enterprise strategy, technology delivery and organisational change. You build trust quickly, bring people with you through complex transformation, and combine ambition with disciplined execution. You are comfortable operating at executive and governance levels while remaining focused on delivering practical outcomes for the people and communities the University serves. You will bring: A relevant postgraduate qualification, deep understanding of technology and systems, and/or substantial experience as a CIO, Chief Digital Officer, Technology Director or in a comparable senior technology leadership role. A track record of designing and delivering large-scale digital transformation aligned to organisational strategy. Experience creating complex, secure and integrated digital ecosystems that deliver an excellent experience for users. Strong knowledge of technology governance, risk, compliance, cyber security and regulatory obligations. Demonstrated experience leading technology investment, financial management, vendor relationships and major transformation portfolios. The ability to influence and engage effectively with senior executives, governing bodies and diverse stakeholder groups. Proven capability to build strong relationships across an organisation and create alignment around strategy, change and major programs. A leadership approach characterised by humility, resilience, accountability and a commitment to developing people and capability. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. Why Charles Sturt? This is an opportunity to make a tangible difference through technology. As Chief Information and Digital Officer, you will help shape the digital foundations of a university with a distinctive role in Australia's rural and regional communities. You will lead a substantial transformation agenda spanning technology, data, cyber security, AI, enterprise architecture and digital services, while helping shape how the University works, teaches, researches and connects into the future. Additional information Fixed-term five-year contract. Attractive salary package, including up to 17% superannuation, with salary packaging options available. Our preference is for the successful candidate to be located at one of our campuses. An alternative eastern-state location may be considered. Relocation assistance provided. Travel will be required. This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents, or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with the position. How to apply Charles Sturt University has engaged Towardco to assist with this important appointment. For a confidential discussion, please contact Terri Ward, Principal at Towardco on 0447 667 439 or via email terri@towardco.com Your application should include a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the Applicant Information Brochure) and your CV. Submit your application to terri@towardco.com quoting CSUCIDO26 in the subject line. Please note that only applications received via this email address will be considered. Applications close at 11pm Monday, 19 October 2026. Advertised: 08 Oct 2026 11:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 19 Oct 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499164/senior-lecturer-associate-professor-in-information-studies": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Information Studies",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Information Studies Apply now Reference no: 499164 Work type: Continuing - Full Time Location: Bathurst, Wagga Wagga Categories: Academic - Teaching and Research Join one of the world's largest Information Studies schools Progress your teaching and research career Flexible working arrangements and relocation assistance available The role Full Time, Continuing Level C - $139,182 to $160,308 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Level D - $167,348 to $184,243 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Bathurst or Wagga Wagga As a Senior Lecturer or Associate Professor in Information Studies, you will play a pivotal role in advancing excellence in teaching, research, and professional engagement within the School of Information and Communication Studies. You will deliver high-quality online teaching across undergraduate and postgraduate programs, supervise Higher Degree by Research candidates, and contribute to curriculum innovation that responds to the evolving needs of the information professions. Through your research leadership and strong industry partnerships, you will help strengthen the University's national and international standing in Information Studies. About you Doctoral qualification in Information Studies or a related discipline Proven success in university teaching, research, and student supervision Strong communication and stakeholder engagement skills. At Senior Lecturer level , you will have a recognised track record of academic achievement and the ability to contribute to leadership across teaching, research, and professional engagement. At Associate Professor level , you will be a nationally or internationally recognised leader with an outstanding research profile, success in attracting funding, and demonstrated experience leading people, partnerships, and research initiatives. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the School of Information and Communication Studies here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position descriptions below: Level C - Senior Lecturer in Information Studies Level D - Associate Professor in Information Studies or by contacting: Recruitment specific enquiries: Madison Petersen | Talent Acquisition Consultant | mapetersen@csu.edu.au Job specific enquiries: Professor Tina Du | Head of School, School of Information and Communication Studies | tdu@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, 5 November 2026 Advertised: 07 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 05 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499168/provicechancellor-first-nations-strategy": {
      "title": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor, First Nations Strategy",
      "description": "Pro-Vice-Chancellor, First Nations Strategy Reference no: 499168 Work type: Fixed Term - Full Time Location: Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie, Wagga Wagga Categories: Executive Full-time | Preferred location: Bathurst (Other Charles Sturt campuses considered) | Executive appointment This position is only open to candidates who identify as Aboriginal and/or Torres Strait Islander. About the Organisation Charles Sturt University is Australia’s largest regional university, shaped by the land, cultures and communities of regional New South Wales. Established as a multi‑campus institution in 1989, Charles Sturt now serves more than 38,000 students across Australia and internationally, with campuses in Albury‑Wodonga, Bathurst, Canberra, Dubbo, Orange, Port Macquarie and Wagga Wagga, as well as metropolitan locations in Sydney and Melbourne. As a university of the land and its people, Charles Sturt is guided by the Wiradjuri phrase Yindyamarra winhanganha which means “the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in.” This ethos shapes the university’s values, culture and aspirations. Charles Sturt has a long-standing commitment to First Nations success and self-determination, working in partnership with communities to create meaningful educational, employment and research outcomes. With more than 2,000 staff and a strong record of First Nations student success, the University is deeply invested in creating culturally safe environments, expanding pathways and embedding First Nations perspectives throughout teaching, research, engagement and operations. As Charles Sturt enters a new decade of transformation through its 2030 Strategy, the University is seeking an outstanding Indigenous leader to help shape the next chapter of this commitment and strengthen its position as Australia’s leading rural and regional university. About the role The Pro Vice-Chancellor, First Nations Strategy is a pivotal Indigenous leadership role that provides strategic direction, cultural authority and high-level advice across the university. Reporting directly to the Vice-Chancellor, the position leads the development, implementation and evaluation of Charles Sturt First Nations Strategy, ensuring First Nations priorities are embedded across all aspects of University life. As a member of the university's senior leadership community, the Pro-Vice-Chancellor provides visible and influential leadership that shapes institutional priorities, drives accountability and champions First Nations excellence across the organisation. The role is highly visible across the university and within community across the Charles Sturt footprint. It requires a leader who can build strong relationships, navigate complexity, influence senior decision-making and provide culturally grounded guidance to executive leaders, faculties, divisions and community stakeholders. The Pro Vice-Chancellor represents the university in matters of significance to First Nations peoples, advocates for improved outcomes, and works collaboratively to strengthen First Nations leadership, student success, research capability and employment pathways. For a full list of responsibilities, please contact Pipeline Talent via jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au . About you This role would be suitable for a senior Indigenous leader from either an academic or professional background who has demonstrated the ability to think and operate at whole‑of‑organisation scale. You bring strong strategic capability, able to shape direction, influence senior decision-making and guide a university‑wide approach to First Nations priorities. You work collaboratively across diverse portfolios, building alignment and uplifting capability so that First Nations strategy is embedded throughout the entire institution. Key Selection Criteria Demonstrated success leading organisation-wide First Nations strategies, initiatives or transformation programs. Established cultural authority, credibility and deep understanding of issues affecting Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples and communities. Strong capability in building collaborative relationships across senior leaders, operational teams and First Nations communities. Experience navigating complex or highly regulated environments to progress strategic priorities. Demonstrated people leadership that supports culturally safe practice and develops capability in others. What we can offer you The opportunity to lead the First Nations Strategy at whole‑of‑university scale and shape cultural capability, engagement and outcomes across Australia’s largest regional university. Enjoy salary packaging and sacrifice options that include novated leasing, self-education expenses, membership to professional associations and child care. Professional development opportunities including leadership development programs for our executive and academic leaders. Reduced cost goods and services through our corporate partners. Access to on-campus childcare services at the Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst and Wagga Wagga campuses, as well as sporting facilities, and private dental clinics at the Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange and Wagga Wagga campuses (standard fees apply). How to apply All applications must include the following: An up-to-date CV A covering letter/pitch (maximum 2 pages) addressing the selection criteria. Applications are to be submitted via the Pipeline Talent website at: https://www.pipelinetalent.com.au/ Applications close at 11:59pm on Sunday, 1 November 2026 How to get more information For further information on the position, please contact the team at Pipeline Talent. Dave Collins Director, Recruitment dave@pipelinetalent.com.au | jobs@pipelinetalent.com.au M 0419 599 251| PH 02 8001 6603 Advertised: 06 Oct 2026 05:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 11:55 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499163/senior-lecturer-associate-professor-in-rural-health": {
      "title": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Rural Health",
      "description": "Senior Lecturer / Associate Professor in Rural Health Apply now Reference no: 499163 Work type: Continuing - Full Time Location: Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange, Wagga Wagga Categories: Academic - Teaching and Research Lead nationally significant rural health education and research Drive innovative education and research in rural healthcare Create lasting impact on regional and rural communities through health workforce initiatives The role Full Time, Continuing Level C - $139,182 to $160,308 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Level D - $167,348 to $184,243 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Albury-Wodonga, Bathurst, Dubbo, Orange or Wagga Wagga We are seeking an accomplished rural health educator and researcher to join the Three Rivers Department of Rural Health (DRH) as an Associate Professor in Rural Health. As an Associate Professor in Rural Health, you will lead rural health education, research, and workforce development initiatives that support healthier regional communities. Working with healthcare, academic, and community partners, you will drive innovative programs, build strategic partnerships, mentor staff, and contribute to strengthening the rural health workforce across regional Australia. Stakeholder engagement and relationship management internally and externally is a key facet of the role. You will initiate and support ongoing stakeholder engagement to promote the objectives of the DRH, whilst also developing capacity for quality clinical training. About you A doctoral qualification relevant to health or equivalent accreditation and standing . A record of significant achievement relevant to rural health, at a regional and national level, in the scholarship of teaching and/or research/creative works or professional activity. Comprehensive experience in, and knowledge of rural health and the Rural Health Multidisciplinary Training Program Demonstrated ability to provide effective leadership and building people capability for high quality student centred learning and rural health research activity and outputs Demonstrated capacity to work both collaboratively, independently and lead in a large complex academic setting with an outcome orientated focus. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of respectfully knowing how to live well in a world worth living in. This phrase represents who we are at Charles Sturt University – our ethos. It comes from traditional Indigenous Australian knowledge, but it also speaks to the mission of universities – to develop and spread wisdom to make the world a better place. Harnessing technology, we thrive as a distributed yet connected community, welcoming and engaging with people across Australia and the world. Learn more about the Faculty of Science and Health here . Learn more about the Three Rivers Department of Rural Health here . Visit our website to learn about our great range of employee benefits . Charles Sturt University is committed to building a diverse workforce as shown through our Athena Swan Bronze Award, our Australian Workplace Equality Index Bronze Award and our membership of a range of diversity focused organisations including Diversity Council Australia and Pride in Diversity. We encourage applications from First Nations people, carers, people from culturally and linguistically diverse backgrounds, people with disability, neurodivergent people, and people of all age groups, genders and sexualities for all roles. To apply Click the Apply button and complete your application online, submit a “statement of suitability” demonstrating your skills, knowledge and/or experience relevant to the position (referencing the selection criteria in the position description) and upload your CV. If you experience difficulties applying online or for further information on completing the application process please visit our how to apply page or contact us . This position is open to Australian Citizens and Permanent Residents; or applicants who hold a current valid work visa commensurate with this position. Disclosure Requirement Applicants may be required to disclose information relating to gender-based violence allegations or findings as part of the University's recruitment and screening processes. Further information Additional information is available in the position description(s) below: Position Description - Senior Lecturer in Rural Health (Clinical Educator) Position Description - Associate Professor in Rural Health or by contacting: Christine Howard | Director - Three Rivers Department of Rural Health | choward@csu.edu.au Closing Date: 11pm, Sunday, 1 November 2026 Advertised: 02 Oct 2026 09:00 AM - AUS Eastern Standard Time Applications close: 01 Nov 2026 11:00 PM - AUS Eastern Daylight Time Apply now Refer a friend Back to search results"
    },
    "https://external-jobs.csu.edu.au/cw/en/job/499169/technical-assistant": {
      "title": "Technical Assistant",
      "description": "Technical Assistant Apply now Reference no: 499169 Work type: Continuing - Part Time Location: Dubbo Categories: Technical Officer, Health Services Great opportunity for early‑career or experienced technical staff Ongoing professional development and university benefits Work with modern labs and specialised equipment The role Continuing, part time (40% fraction) Level 4 - $29,292 - $31,737 pa (plus 17% superannuation) Dubbo We are seeking a committed Technical Assistant to provide high‑quality technical and laboratory support across teaching, clinical and research environments. In this hands-on role, you will work within a collaborative Technical Services team, supporting classes in areas such as Biomedical Sciences, Chemistry, Microbiology, Healthcare Science and Nursing. This is a great opportunity for someone who thrives in a practical lab‑based environment, and looks to work collaboratively across multiple campuses and disciplines as part of a multi-skilled technical team. There are physical capability requirements inherent to this position thus as part of this recruitment, a pre-employment functional screening is required. About you Qualifications, skills and experience relevant to the role Knowledge and experience in chemistry, microbiology, anatomy & physiology, or related technical disciplines. Strong organisational skills with the ability to work accurately in a high‑volume environment. Ability to work both independently and as part of a cooperative, efficient team. Ability to follow laboratory procedures and maintain high safety standards. Experience in maintaining records for equipment, chemicals and consumables. About us We are a university of the land and people of our regions. True to the character of regional Australia we have gumption, we have soul and we collaborate with others. We develop holistic, far-sighted people who help their communities grow and flourish. The Wiradjuri phrase yindyamarra winhanganha means the wisdom of 